' Pattern component notes
note left of Singleton
  <b>Private Constructor</b>
  Prevents external instantiation.
  Only accessible within the class.

  <b>Example:</b>
  private Singleton() {
      // Initialize resources
      this.data = new Object();
  }
end note

note top of Singleton
  <b>Static Volatile Instance</b>
  • volatile ensures visibility across threads
  • Prevents instruction reordering
  • Essential for double-checked locking

  <b>Why volatile?</b>
  Without volatile, another thread might see
  a partially constructed object.
end note

note right of Singleton
  <b>Double-Checked Locking Pattern</b>

  public static Singleton getInstance() {
      // First check (no locking)
      if (instance == null) {
          // Synchronize on class object
          synchronized (Singleton.class) {
              // Second check (with lock)
              if (instance == null) {
                  instance = new Singleton();
              }
          }
      }
      return instance;
  }

  <b>How it works:</b>
  1. First check: Fast path for already-initialized instance
  2. Synchronization: Only when instance is null
  3. Second check: Prevents race condition
  4. Return: Thread-safe singleton instance

  <b>Benefits:</b>
  • Thread-safe initialization
  • Minimal synchronization overhead
  • Lazy initialization
  • Good performance after initialization
end note

note right of Singleton
  <b>Thread Safety Guarantee:</b>

  <b>Scenario:</b> Two threads (T1, T2) call getInstance() simultaneously

  <b>Without Double-Check:</b>
  T1: if (instance == null) → true
  T2: if (instance == null) → true
  T1: creates instance
  T2: creates another instance ✗ (BUG!)

  <b>With Double-Check:</b>
  T1: if (instance == null) → true
  T2: if (instance == null) → true
  T1: acquires lock, checks again, creates instance
  T2: waits for lock
  T2: acquires lock, checks again → false (instance exists)
  T2: returns existing instance ✓ (SAFE!)

  <b>Performance:</b>
  • First call: Slight overhead due to synchronization
  • Subsequent calls: No synchronization (just null check)
  • Much faster than synchronized method approach
end note

' Legend
note right of Singleton
  <b>Singleton Pattern Core Components:</b>

  <b>Private Constructor:</b> Prevents direct
  instantiation from outside the class.

  <b>Static Instance Variable:</b> Holds the
  single instance. Declared volatile for
  thread safety.

  <b>Static getInstance() Method:</b> Provides
  global access point with thread-safe
  lazy initialization.

  <b>Double-Checked Locking:</b>
  • Checks instance == null twice
  • Synchronizes only on first initialization
  • Minimizes performance impact
  • Requires volatile keyword (Java 5+)

  <b>Key Benefits:</b>
  • Lazy initialization (created when needed)
  • Thread-safe (prevents multiple instances)
  • High performance (minimal locking)
  • Memory efficient (single instance)

  <b>When to Use:</b>
  • Need exactly one instance
  • Global access point required
  • Thread safety is important
  • Resource-heavy object (lazy init beneficial)
end note

